  "id": "ex-12-modalverben-a2",
  "intro": "Standalone drill set, not tied to any lesson. Unlike most verbs, modal verbs almost always use the Präteritum even in spoken German (ich musste gehen, not ich habe gehen müssen) — so this is the one Präteritum you need for everyday conversation, not just writing. The forms drop the umlaut from the infinitive (können → konnte, müssen → musste, dürfen → durfte) and take regular weak-verb endings. One trap: möchten has no Präteritum of its own — for \"wanted to\" in the past you switch to wollte, while mochte (from mögen) means \"liked\", a different meaning entirely. Blocks: A Basistraining → B Vertiefung → C Sprachbausteine → D Wiederholung. Answers in solutions.md.\n",
  "lesson": "EX/12-modalverben-a2",
  "level": "A2",
  "summary": null,
  "title": "Modalverben im Präteritum — A2",
